course: dictionary
lesson: 1763
title: Clearly: easy to see or obviously true
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Clearly» has two main meanings. The first is in a way that is easy to see, hear or understand, as in «Speak clearly» so that everyone can follow you.
explain: The second is obviously, without any doubt, as in «She was clearly upset». You can tell from her face and voice, so the writer thinks it is obvious.
explain: Writers often use «clearly» when they think something is obvious. Be careful, though: an obvious thing can still be wrong, so check it against the facts before you believe it.
fact: «Clearly» means in a way that is easy to see, hear or understand.
fact: «Clearly» can also mean obviously, without doubt.
fact: «Speak clearly» means say your words so others can hear and understand them.
fact: «Clearly marked» means marked so you can easily see it.
fact: When a writer says «clearly», check the evidence, because obvious things can be wrong.
exercise: «Speak clearly so everyone can hear you.» What does clearly mean here? | Loudly and angrily | Very quickly and quietly | With your eyes closed | *In a way that is easy to hear and understand
exercise: «She was clearly upset.» What does clearly mean here? | For a short time only | Quietly, so nobody hears | *Obviously, without any doubt | Slowly and carefully
exercise: «Set out your maths working clearly so others can follow it.» Find and copy the word that tells how to set out your working. | others | *clearly | follow | maths
test: «The exit is clearly marked, so we can find it quickly.» What does clearly marked mean? | Marked late by a teacher | *Marked so it is easy to see | Hidden under leaves | Marked in red paint only
test: Why should you say clearly where it hurts when you visit the doctor? | Because doctors like loud voices | *So the doctor can understand and help you | So the doctor will not ask questions | Because the doctor can feel your pain for you
exam: A story says, «clearly, the hero was right». Why should you check this claim before you believe it? | Because clearly is a question word | *Because obvious things can still be wrong | Because clearly always means happy | Because it is a very long word
